    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis (JIA) is a complex genetic disease characterized by chronic inflammation of the joints. The primary molecular phenotypes include a 40-fold increase in proinflammatory cytokines such as IL-1β and IL-6. A computational analysis was conducted on a cohort of 18 patient exomes, 14 of which were accompanied with exomes of unaffected parents and siblings. Compared to a healthy control population, the patients had an enrichment of single nucleotide variants in ATN1, ZDBF2, and TNRC18 genes. A large fraction (5/14) of the patients also showed de novo variants in SIRPα, which regulates immune response suppression and phagocytosis inhibition, explaining many hallmark phenotypes of JIA.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
